1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,550
What is a stash?

2
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:09,715
The stash is a shelf for unfinished work.

3
00:00:09,715 --> 00:00:15,200
git stash puts your uncommitted changes aside and gives you a clean working directory,

4
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:18,899
so you can switch to something urgent and come back later.

5
00:00:20,633 --> 00:00:25,033
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

6
00:00:25,567 --> 00:00:28,262
You are halfway through adding a new drink:

7
00:00:28,262 --> 00:00:28,700
Urgent:

8
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:35,281
the espresso price on main is wrong and must be fixed now, without your half-finished work in the commit.

9
00:00:35,833 --> 00:00:36,327
Urgent:

10
00:00:36,327 --> 00:00:43,732
the espresso price on main is wrong and must be fixed now, without your half-finished work in the commit.

11
00:00:43,732 --> 00:00:45,143
The folder is clean.

12
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:47,490
The folder is clean.

13
00:00:47,490 --> 00:00:50,445
Fix and commit the urgent change:

14
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:53,949
Let me try something.

15
00:00:53,949 --> 00:00:57,364
Stash when your work includes a new file:

16
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:03,085
The modified tracked files were stashed, but specials.txt is still there:

17
00:01:03,085 --> 00:01:06,281
by default git stash ignores untracked files.

18
00:01:06,281 --> 00:01:12,390
If you now switch branches or reset, the new file travels with you or gets in the way.

19
00:01:14,133 --> 00:01:19,298
Pop the stash, then stash again with -u (include untracked):

20
00:01:21,033 --> 00:01:21,747
Got it.

21
00:01:21,747 --> 00:01:24,498
What do I need to remember?

22
00:01:25,033 --> 00:01:31,553
git stash shelves uncommitted work and cleans the working directory; git stash pop brings it back.

23
00:01:32,100 --> 00:01:35,050
Untracked files need git stash -u.

24
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:40,790
Stashes are local commits under refs/stash; they are never pushed.

25
00:01:42,533 --> 00:01:46,547
I keep hearing about stash, apply and pop.

26
00:01:46,547 --> 00:01:48,363
What is it, really?

27
00:01:48,900 --> 00:01:55,267
The difference between apply (re-apply, keep the stash) and pop (re-apply and delete it),

28
00:01:55,267 --> 00:02:01,419
how to give stashes names, and what happens when a stash conflicts with newer commits.

29
00:02:03,167 --> 00:02:07,566
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

30
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:11,706
Stash with a message, so you recognise it later:

31
00:02:11,706 --> 00:02:16,889
apply the same stash onto two branches, because it is useful on both:

32
00:02:17,433 --> 00:02:22,496
apply the same stash onto two branches, because it is useful on both:

33
00:02:22,496 --> 00:02:25,578
After two applys the stash is still there.

34
00:02:25,578 --> 00:02:27,633
Remove it when you are done:

35
00:02:28,167 --> 00:02:31,340
After two applys the stash is still there.

36
00:02:31,340 --> 00:02:33,456
Remove it when you are done:

37
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:36,707
Let me try something.

38
00:02:36,707 --> 00:02:43,310
Pop a stash onto a version of the file that changed since you stashed it, on the same lines:

39
00:02:43,867 --> 00:02:49,687
The stash's chai line was added right after green tea, which was changed since:

40
00:02:49,687 --> 00:02:52,192
a conflict, resolved like a merge.

41
00:02:52,192 --> 00:02:53,592
Note the last line:

42
00:02:53,592 --> 00:02:57,276
The stash entry is kept in case you need it again.

43
00:02:59,033 --> 00:03:06,358
Resolve (keep both lines), mark resolved, and drop the stash yourself since pop did not:

44
00:03:08,100 --> 00:03:08,813
Got it.

45
00:03:08,813 --> 00:03:11,564
What do I need to remember?

46
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:17,169
apply keeps the stash, pop removes it (unless it conflicts).

47
00:03:17,733 --> 00:03:23,033
Name stashes with -m; stash single files with git stash push FILE.

48
00:03:23,567 --> 00:03:29,036
Stash conflicts are resolved like merge conflicts; then git stash drop.

49
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:33,474
How does managing stashes work?

50
00:03:34,033 --> 00:03:37,251
How to keep several stashes under control:

51
00:03:37,251 --> 00:03:41,235
list them, see what is inside, apply a specific one,

52
00:03:41,235 --> 00:03:46,828
turn one into a branch, clean up, and recover a stash dropped by mistake.

53
00:03:48,567 --> 00:03:52,966
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

54
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:55,180
Inspect before using:

55
00:03:55,180 --> 00:03:58,940
show without -p only shows the file statistics.

56
00:03:58,940 --> 00:04:02,219
Turn the older idea into a proper branch:

57
00:04:02,767 --> 00:04:05,995
show without -p only shows the file statistics.

58
00:04:05,995 --> 00:04:08,811
Turn the older idea into a proper branch:

59
00:04:08,811 --> 00:04:15,886
git stash branch created price-draft at the commit where the stash was made, applied it and dropped it.

60
00:04:17,633 --> 00:04:19,335
Let me try something.

61
00:04:19,335 --> 00:04:23,953
Clear the shelf, then realise one stash was still needed:

62
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:29,357
git stash clear removed the refs/stash entries, but stashes are commits:

63
00:04:29,357 --> 00:04:36,036
the objects are still in the repository until Git's garbage collection removes unreachable objects.

64
00:04:36,036 --> 00:04:41,365
git fsck lists unreachable commits; stash commits have the message On <branch>:

65
00:04:41,365 --> 00:04:42,039
<message>:

66
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:45,599
Here is the fix.

67
00:04:47,333 --> 00:04:48,047
Got it.

68
00:04:48,047 --> 00:04:50,798
What do I need to remember?

69
00:04:51,333 --> 00:04:57,253
git stash list / show -p to know what you have; apply a specific stash@{N}.

70
00:04:57,800 --> 00:05:03,764
git stash branch turns a stash into a branch, avoiding conflicts with a moved main.

71
00:05:04,300 --> 00:05:07,091
Dropped stashes are unreachable commits:

72
00:05:07,091 --> 00:05:10,929
git fsck --unreachable can still find them for a while.
